On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:35:08 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Which makes me wonder - is it possible to simplify even the patch
> creation, by using git-format-patch(1) and then writing additional
> information into it? Maybe that's what it already does, I'm not sure.

That is what it already does. format-patch.sh runs git format-patch
with every argument you give it, then makes one awk pass over each file
it wrote. That pass adds the commit's change id twice: as a Change-Id
header, and as a "change-id:" line under the "---". Everything else
comes from git itself: base-commit, the cover letter, -v N, threading,
and the interdiff.

I need to correct my last message there. I said the wrapper adds the
interdiff and that plain git format-patch cannot. That is wrong. git
format-patch --interdiff=<rev> writes it. The wrapper's only part in
it, in my unmerged "libexec/mail: Refuse a reroll without an
interdiff", is to rebuild the previous version from the list so git
has a <rev> to compare against.

The change id is the one thing git cannot add on its own.
--add-header puts the same header on every patch, and git does not
copy jj's change-id commit header into the patch. So the wrapper is
already as small as it can be. I recommend keeping it as is.

> Also the tool should enforce that the input is a correctly formatted
